course: euphemisms
lesson: 1
title: Soft words for hard things: death and dying
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A euphemism is a soft or roundabout word we use instead of a plain word that feels too hard, rude or sad.
explain: Death is the thing people soften most: at a funeral nobody says "Grandad is dead", they say "Grandad passed away" or "he is no longer with us", to be kind to the family.
explain: Other words for death do the opposite and joke about it: "kicked the bucket", "croaked" or "pushing up daisies" are rough and funny, fine for a dead goldfish or a film villain but hurtful at a real funeral.
explain: So the same fact, "he died", can be said kindly, formally, in old-fashioned words or as a joke, and a clever listener hears both what happened and how the speaker feels about it.
explain: Vivid example: when a teacher says "Sam's nan passed on at the weekend", nobody went anywhere; she died, and the teacher chose a gentle word.
fact: "Passed away" means died; it is the polite, kind word.
fact: "Passed on" means died; it is polite and gentle.
fact: "Passed", said alone ("his dad passed last year"), means died; it is common in everyday American speech.
fact: "Departed this life" means died; it is old-fashioned and found on old gravestones.
fact: "Kicked the bucket" means died; it is a joking, informal phrase.
fact: "Bought the farm" means died, often in a crash or in battle; it is American slang.
fact: "Pushing up daisies" means dead and buried; it is a joke about flowers growing on a grave.
fact: "Six feet under" means dead and buried, because graves were dug about six feet deep.
fact: "Bit the dust" means died, or was destroyed or broke; it is slang.
fact: "Croaked" means died; it is rough slang.
fact: "Shuffled off this mortal coil" means died; it comes from Shakespeare's Hamlet and is used jokingly.
fact: "Gone to a better place" means died; it is a kind phrase, often religious.
fact: "Lost" in "she lost her husband" means her husband died; nothing was mislaid.
fact: "No longer with us" means dead, not that the person moved away.
fact: "At rest" means dead; it is written on graves and in sympathy cards.
fact: "Gone to meet his maker" means died; "his maker" is God, and the phrase is often used jokingly.
fact: "Breathed his last" means died; it is old-fashioned and solemn.
fact: "The late" before a name ("the late Mr Jones") means that the person is dead; it does not mean he came late.
fact: "Laid to rest" means buried.
fact: "Final resting place" means a person's grave.
exercise: What does "passed away" mean when a doctor says it to a family? | The person died | Fell asleep | Went to a ward | Fainted
exercise: "My aunt passed on in May." What happened to the aunt? | She died in May | She moved | She retired | She passed by
exercise: An American says "his dad passed last year". What does "passed" mean here? | His dad died last year | His dad passed a test | His dad walked past | His dad changed jobs last year
exercise: An old gravestone says "departed this life 1871". What does that mean? | The person died in 1871 | Moved away in 1871 | Was born in 1871 | Got married in 1871
exercise: What does "kicked the bucket" mean? | Died, in a joking way | Knocked over a pail | Lost his temper | Kicked off a game
exercise: In an American war film, "the pilot bought the farm" means what? | The pilot was killed | He bought land | The pilot retired | He landed on a farm
exercise: "Old Barney's been pushing up daisies for years." What is true of Barney? | He is dead and buried | He is a keen gardener | He works on a flower farm | He has been ill for years
exercise: What does "six feet under" mean? | Dead and buried | Very short | Deep in a cave | Hiding in a cellar
exercise: "Another villain bit the dust." What happened to him? | He died or was defeated | He fell in the dirt | He ate some sand | He ran away from town
exercise: What does it mean if someone says the old toad in the cartoon "croaked"? | The old toad died | It made a frog noise | It caught a cold | It jumped away
exercise: In a joke, "he shuffled off this mortal coil". What did he do? | He died | He left the house | He got out of bed | He untangled a spring
exercise: "Granny has gone to a better place." What does this usually mean? | Granny has died | Granny moved | Granny went on holiday | Granny went into a care home
exercise: "She lost her husband last winter." What does "lost" mean here? | Her husband died | She could not find him | They had a fight | He left her for good
exercise: "Mr Patel is no longer with us." In a sad speech this means what? | Mr Patel has died | Mr Patel left the company | He is on holiday | Mr Patel is in another room
exercise: A sympathy card says "Now he is at rest." What does it mean? | He is dead | He is sleeping | He retired from work | He is on a break
exercise: "He's gone to meet his maker." What does this mean? | He has died | He has visited his parents | He has gone to a factory | He went out for a walk
exercise: "She breathed her last at dawn." What happened? | She died at dawn | She took a deep breath at dawn | She woke up | She stopped talking at dawn
exercise: What does "the late Mrs Brown" tell you? | Mrs Brown is dead | Mrs Brown is always late | She came late | Mrs Brown works night shifts
exercise: "The soldier was laid to rest on Friday." What happened on Friday? | He was buried | He went to bed early | He slept all day | He was put in hospital
exercise: "This churchyard is the poet's final resting place." What is it? | The place where the poet is buried | The place where the poet liked to nap | The place where the poet retired | The last place the poet visited
test: News: "The singer, who passed away on Sunday, will be buried in her home town." What happened on Sunday? | The singer died | The singer gave her last concert | The singer went back home | She fell ill on stage
test: Mia: "my goldfish croaked lol, flushing him tonight." Is Mia sad about a real person? | No, she is joking roughly about her dead fish | Yes, a person in her family died | No, her fish is making noises | Yes, her fish is sick but alive
test: At a funeral, which would hurt the family most? | "Sorry he kicked the bucket" | "Sorry he passed away" | "Sorry he is no longer with us" | "Sorry for your loss"
test: Gossip: "Old Fred? Six feet under since March, mate." What do you learn about Fred? | He died and was buried in March | He went to live underground in March | He fell into a hole in March | He moved to a small flat in March
test: Ben's friend says "the late Mr Hill built this school". Ben says "so he's always late?" What should you tell Ben? | No, "the late" means Mr Hill has died | Yes, he was never on time | No, it means he built it late at night | Yes, the school was finished late
test: A neighbour says "We lost our dad in the spring." What should you say? | "I'm so sorry he died" | "Did you find him?" | "Where did you lose him?" | "Was he lost in the park?"
test: In a western: "Three outlaws bit the dust in the shootout." What happened? | Three outlaws were killed | They ate dinner | Three outlaws fell off horses | Three outlaws ran into the desert
test: Mum: "Your great-grandad departed this life before you were born." What does she mean? | He died before you were born | He left the country before you were born | He stopped working before you were born | He was born before you were
test: A plaque in a park reads "Here lies Captain Grey, at rest at last." What is under the plaque? | Captain Grey's grave | Captain Grey's bed | Captain Grey's old boat | A bench for Captain Grey
test: Tom: "Grandpa says when he goes to meet his maker I get his watch." When will Tom get the watch? | When Grandpa dies | When Grandpa visits his own dad | When it is made | When Grandpa finishes making it
test: Chatty: "Bloody car finally bit the dust on the motorway." What happened? | The car broke down for good | The car got covered in dust | The car hit a man | The car was washed
test: A US pilot story: "Two of his friends bought the farm over the sea." What happened to his friends? | They were killed over the sea | They bought farms by the sea | They moved to the country | They landed on a farm safely
exam: Identify the plain meaning of the expression "passed away" as employed in a formal notice of bereavement. | The person has died | Relocated to another address | Was discharged from hospital | Lost consciousness briefly
exam: It is given that a churchyard is described as the "final resting place" of a certain individual. Determine what is thereby asserted. | The individual is buried there | The individual habitually slept there | The individual last resided there | The individual worked there
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "kicked the bucket". | An informal and humorous way of stating that a person died | A formal and respectful way of stating that a person died | A literal description of upsetting a vessel of water | A term denoting the commencement of a sporting contest
exam: Ascertain the meaning conveyed by the designation "the late" when it precedes the name of a person. | That the person is deceased | That the person is habitually unpunctual | That the person is late | That the person works during the night
